From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nyxloom-carver <development@richtfest.tech>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 13:27:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Skip the pre-boot chown when it would change nothing

Chown walks a server's whole data directory and calls Lchownat on every
entry unconditionally. That has two costs.

A chown dirties the inode and journals the change even when the ownership
is already correct, so booting a server with a large data directory does a
great deal of write work that changes nothing.

More seriously, a chown against a read-only mount fails with EROFS whether
or not the requested ownership already matches -- the kernel does not
special-case a no-op. Any read-only bind mount inside a server's data
directory therefore makes the server impossible to boot, which rules out
otherwise reasonable setups: shared immutable game content mounted once and
reused by several servers, or a read-only network mount.

Skip the syscall when it provably changes nothing. The condition is
narrower than "the uid and gid already match": for anything that is not a
directory the kernel also clears the setuid and setgid bits on chown, and
does so even when the ids are unchanged, so an entry still carrying either
bit is chowned unconditionally and that behaviour is preserved.

The entry's ownership comes from the DirEntry the walker already yields, so
this trades one lchownat for one fstatat on the unchanged path.
---
 server/filesystem/filesystem.go | 40 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 40 insertions(+)

diff --git a/server/filesystem/filesystem.go b/server/filesystem/filesystem.go
index 112f996..60964a6 100644
--- a/server/filesystem/filesystem.go
+++ b/server/filesystem/filesystem.go
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@ import (
 	"github.com/apex/log"
 	"github.com/gabriel-vasile/mimetype"
 	ignore "github.com/sabhiram/go-gitignore"
+	"golang.org/x/sys/unix"
 
 	"github.com/pterodactyl/wings/config"
 	"github.com/pterodactyl/wings/internal/ufs"
@@ -284,6 +285,9 @@ func (fs *Filesystem) Chown(p string) error {
 		if err != nil {
 			return err
 		}
+		if chownIsNoop(info, uid, gid) {
+			return nil
+		}
 		if err := fs.unixFS.Lchownat(dirfd, name, uid, gid); err != nil {
 			return err
 		}
@@ -294,6 +298,42 @@ func (fs *Filesystem) Chown(p string) error {
 	return nil
 }
 
+// chownIsNoop reports whether chowning the entry to uid:gid would change
+// nothing observable, allowing the walk in Chown to skip the syscall.
+//
+// Skipping matters for two reasons. A chown dirties the inode (and journals
+// the change) even when the ownership is unchanged, so walking a large server
+// root on every boot is far more expensive than it needs to be. And a chown
+// against a read-only mount fails with EROFS regardless of whether the
+// requested ownership already matches, which makes any read-only bind mount
+// inside a server's data directory — shared immutable game content, a
+// read-only network mount — impossible to boot with.
+//
+// The check is deliberately narrower than "the uid and gid already match".
+// For anything that is not a directory the kernel also clears the setuid and
+// setgid bits on chown, and does so even when the ids are unchanged, so an
+// entry still carrying either bit is chowned unconditionally to preserve that
+// behaviour. A pre-existing "security.capability" attribute is not inspected:
+// setting one requires CAP_SETFCAP, which no path Wings offers a server can
+// obtain, so it cannot appear on content Wings is responsible for.
+func chownIsNoop(info ufs.DirEntry, uid, gid int) bool {
+	if info == nil {
+		return false
+	}
+	st, err := info.Info()
+	if err != nil {
+		return false
+	}
+	sys, ok := st.Sys().(*unix.Stat_t)
+	if !ok {
+		return false
+	}
+	if int(sys.Uid) != uid || int(sys.Gid) != gid {
+		return false
+	}
+	return st.IsDir() || st.Mode()&(ufs.ModeSetuid|ufs.ModeSetgid) == 0
+}
+
 func (fs *Filesystem) Chmod(path string, mode ufs.FileMode) error {
 	return fs.unixFS.Chmod(path, mode)
 }
